    "text": "The dispatch office was a bunker of light and silence, buried in the night. The only sounds were the low, constant hum of the monitors and the occasional crackle of a radio. Elias had worked the graveyard shift for twelve years, and the quiet had become a part of him, a second skin.\n\nHis world was a wall of glowing screens, a constellation of green and amber dots crawling across digital maps. Each dot was a train, a sleeping giant of steel and freight, and he was their shepherd. He knew their rhythms, their weights, their sighs.\n\nAt 3:17 AM, the system flagged a minor anomaly on the old Northline track, a route now used only for maintenance and the rare, slow-moving bulk train. It was a sensor ghost, a flicker that would likely clear itself, but protocol was protocol. He reached for the backup binder, a thick, dusty tome of laminated timetables that predated the digital age.\n\nHe only used it for cross-referencing old mile markers. The pages were stiff, and they smelled of ozone and old paper. As he flipped through the Northline section, his thumb caught on something. A small, folded piece of paper, tucked deep in the crease between two pages. It wasn't the crisp, yellowed paper of the timetable itself, but a softer, whiter stock, like a page from a pocket notebook.\n\nHe carefully teased it out. The handwriting was neat, slanted, and done in blue ink.\n\n*To whoever is watching the Northline tonight,*\n\n*If you see",
